  "summary_text": "Kyle, here's your Friday read.\n\nPort Washington crushed it yesterday\u2014$4,566 revenue on 7 active groomers yielding $506 per groomer, a tick above your 30-day baseline. This is clean execution: core grooms up 67% week-over-week, add-on attach rate climbed to 54%, transaction count up 38%. You've got real momentum in week one of August. The lapse pool of 469 customers representing $296K in annual value is a rich reactivation opportunity; lean into that.\n\nHicksville reversed course. Down 21% yesterday to $1,283 on just 3 active groomers producing $405 per groomer\u20143% below baseline. The damage is disproportionate in retail, which tanked 77% while core grooms only dropped 8%. This is a front-of-house signal, not a grooming problem. Add-on attach actually improved to 54%, so upsell discipline is there. Week-to-date Hicksville is still up 10%, but yesterday's retail miss deserves a conversation with your floor team about POS visibility and customer engagement at checkout.\n\nBoth stores show the same inventory pressure: X-Small Treat Table is critically short (zero stock in Port Washington, negative 140 units in Hicksville), and popular items like the braided bully sticks are in negative inventory. Reorder those immediately; you're losing transaction opportunities. Port Washington's 20-item out-of-stock list is manageable, but Hicksville's dual shortage in small paper bags and supply items suggests a restocking gap.\n\nPort Washington today: 4 groomer-days scheduled with 8.0 bather hours; Hicksville: 2 groomer-days, 0 bather hours; next 7 days: 51 combined groomer-days across both stores."
